Object.assign(window.LESSON_CONTENT || (window.LESSON_CONTENT = {}), {"y10-11-media-studies-t1-w1":{"overview":"Students will begin the course by establishing respectful discussion and practical production routines. They will identify media forms, purposes and audiences and learn to support audience claims with observable evidence. Students will then analyse how visual and written choices create meaning, distinguishing description from interpretation and recognising that the same text can be read differently by different viewers.","assessment":"Teacher questioning, class discussion, analysis of worked examples and the completed independent activities. Check that students use accurate terminology, select specific evidence and explain how a choice creates meaning or addresses an audience. Review the written, visual or practical work alongside its annotations, and use the final checks in both lessons to identify the next skill requiring practice.","lessons":[{"date":"25 August 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to explain classroom production routines and identify how media products address particular audiences.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Display a film still, a magazine cover and a television opening. Ask students who might use each product and what evidence supports their suggestion. Invite different interpretations before introducing the course vocabulary."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Introduce a media text as a constructed product that communicates meaning to an audience. Explain the distinction between describing a product and analysing how it addresses viewers. Establish equipment care, organised file naming, respectful discussion and permission before photographing or recording someone."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Use a teacher-prepared school-club announcement to identify its producer, intended audience and purpose. Model how a particular image or phrase offers evidence of audience appeal. Demonstrate saving the analysis in the class folder and explain how production roles help a group work efficiently."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students classify three selected media products by form, purpose and likely audience, giving one observable reason for each choice. Pairs then agree production routines for a small classroom task, identify their responsibilities and save their completed audience table using the agreed filename."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to distinguish an audience inference from an unsupported guess. Check the saved table and invite each pair to explain one routine that protects equipment, respects participants or makes their production work easier to manage."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide an audience table with form, purpose, audience and evidence headings. Model the first entry and offer vocabulary prompts. Let students rehearse an explanation with a partner before writing it, while checking that both students contribute."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students identify a possible secondary audience and explain how the same product could appeal differently to two groups. Ask them what further evidence would be needed to confirm an audience claim."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"28 August 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to analyse how visual and written choices in a media text create meaning for an audience.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Show a still image with its caption hidden, then reveal the caption. Ask whether the suggested meaning changes and which detail led students to revise their first interpretation."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Introduce media language through image, colour, setting, costume and written language. Explain the difference between what is directly visible and what an image may suggest. Emphasise that interpretations need evidence and that different audiences may respond differently to the same detail."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Annotate a fictional school-event image together. Describe the foreground subject and the accompanying words before explaining how framing and caption direct attention. Model a paragraph that connects a specific feature, a suggested meaning and a possible audience response without claiming that every viewer reacts identically."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students annotate a second teacher-selected image and write a short analysis of two choices. They exchange work with a partner who underlines the evidence and checks whether the suggested meaning follows from it. Students revise one unsupported or overly general statement."},{"label":"C:","text":"Invite two interpretations of the same image and compare their evidence. Collect the annotated text and check that students explain how a feature creates meaning rather than only listing colours, objects or personal preferences."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a description-and-interpretation grid and sentence starters such as “The image shows…” and “This may suggest…”. Model one complete connection before students analyse a different feature independently."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students explain how a different caption or crop could change the meaning while retaining the original image. They justify an alternative audience reading using a specific visual detail."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["GCSE Media Studies for AQA (Heinemann): Introduction; Unit 1 Film: audience and media language","Teacher computer, display screen and speakers where audio is used","Age-appropriate teacher-selected extracts or teacher-prepared fictional media texts","Prepared analysis worksheet, evidence grid or production brief","Paper, pencils, storyboard or design templates, and whiteboard markers","Classroom computers and save folders; camera or approved image bank when required","Lesson success criteria and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-media-studies-t1-w2":{"overview":"Students will develop their understanding of genre and the recurring conventions used in film. They will examine how setting, character, visual style and sound establish expectations, recognising that a film may combine or challenge conventions. Students will compare two short examples using matched features and explain similarities and differences through evidence rather than relying on genre labels alone.","assessment":"Teacher questioning, class discussion, analysis of worked examples and the completed independent activities. Check that students use accurate terminology, select specific evidence and explain how a choice creates meaning or addresses an audience. Review the written, visual or practical work alongside its annotations, and use the final checks in both lessons to identify the next skill requiring practice.","lessons":[{"date":"1 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to identify film genre conventions and explain how they establish audience expectations.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Show contrasting teacher-selected film stills without their titles. Ask students to suggest a genre and identify the setting, character or visual detail that influenced their decision. Record disagreements as questions to investigate."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain genre as a category recognised through recurring conventions. Discuss typical settings, character roles, narrative situations, costume, props, lighting and sound. Distinguish a convention from a rule that every film must follow, and explain that genres can overlap or change."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Analyse an age-appropriate science-fiction example using the book’s approach to reading film language. Identify evidence such as an unfamiliar setting or technology and connect it to audience expectations about the story. Compare a clear convention with a feature that alone would not establish genre."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students complete a genre-evidence grid for three selected stills or short extracts. For each example, they identify at least three conventions and explain one expectation created for the audience. Groups compare their choices and revise a label that is not supported by the available evidence."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to explain one convention and why it helps an audience recognise a genre. Check that their evidence comes from the text rather than from knowing its title or remembering unrelated information about the film."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a genre vocabulary bank and a grid divided into setting, character, visual style and sound. Complete one row together and pause an extract at useful moments so students can gather evidence before explaining it."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students identify a possible genre hybrid and explain which conventions come from each genre. Ask how combining familiar conventions with an unexpected feature could attract an audience."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"4 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to compare how two short film examples use genre conventions to appeal to audiences.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Return to two contrasting examples from the previous lesson. Ask whether sharing a genre necessarily means that both texts create the same mood or appeal to the same viewers."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain comparison as examining both texts against the same feature rather than writing two unrelated descriptions. Discuss how conventions can be repeated, adapted or challenged. Encourage qualified audience claims supported by the extracts, rather than assuming that a genre belongs to one age group or gender."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Model a comparison of setting and sound in two age-appropriate extracts. Identify a similarity, explain a significant difference and connect it to the expectation or mood created. Show how a useful comparison refers to specific evidence from both examples within the same paragraph."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Pairs gather evidence in a shared comparison grid, then individually write two linked paragraphs comparing the examples. Each paragraph should address one feature in both texts and explain an audience effect. Students use peer feedback to improve a comparison that simply lists observations."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to read a sentence that compares rather than describes. Check that both examples appear in the response and that the claimed similarity or difference is supported by identifiable details."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide matched prompts for each extract and a paragraph frame using “Both…”, “However…” and “This may…”. Allow students to select from a small number of relevant conventions before choosing their own comparison focus."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students evaluate which example more strongly signals its genre and justify the judgement. They consider whether familiarity with genre conventions might change an audience’s interpretation."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["GCSE Media Studies for AQA (Heinemann): Unit 1 Film: Genre and reading film language","Teacher computer, display screen and speakers where audio is used","Age-appropriate teacher-selected extracts or teacher-prepared fictional media texts","Prepared analysis worksheet, evidence grid or production brief","Paper, pencils, storyboard or design templates, and whiteboard markers","Classroom computers and save folders; camera or approved image bank when required","Lesson success criteria and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-media-studies-t1-w3":{"overview":"Students will examine how narrative structure organises a story and creates audience interest. They will apply the book’s narrative-stage model to a short example, identifying a disruption and the attempts to resolve it. Students will then plan an original short narrative as a storyboard, using ordered images and production notes to make its events understandable to another person.","assessment":"Teacher questioning, class discussion, analysis of worked examples and the completed independent activities. Check that students use accurate terminology, select specific evidence and explain how a choice creates meaning or addresses an audience. Review the written, visual or practical work alongside its annotations, and use the final checks in both lessons to identify the next skill requiring practice.","lessons":[{"date":"8 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to explain narrative structure and identify how a disruption and its resolution organise a story.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Tell a brief fictional story about a lost school key and stop before the ending. Ask what information students already know, what problem has appeared and what questions they expect the ending to answer."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Introduce the book’s account of Todorov’s narrative stages: equilibrium, disruption, recognition, attempted repair and a new equilibrium. Explain that the model is a way to analyse many stories rather than a rule followed by every text. Distinguish the order of events from the way a story presents them."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Map the lost-key story onto the stages. Identify the moment when the problem becomes clear and explain how attempts to solve it move the narrative forward. Discuss how the ending changes the opening situation and demonstrate why a stage may occupy more than one scene."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students analyse a short teacher-selected narrative or synopsis using a stage-and-evidence table. They identify the central problem, support each stage with an event and discuss any point that does not fit neatly. Each student writes an explanation of how the disruption creates audience interest."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to distinguish recognition of a problem from an attempt to repair it. Check that their examples are actual story events and that they can explain how the ending relates to the opening."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide the stages on separate cards and a simplified synopsis. Model the first two matches and use prompts asking what changes, who notices it and what action follows. Accept an oral explanation before the written response."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students explain how a flashback or an unresolved ending could alter the audience’s experience. They distinguish changing the presentation order from changing the underlying sequence of events."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"11 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to storyboard a short narrative using a clear sequence of images and production notes.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Show a short written synopsis beside an unlabelled set of storyboard frames. Ask what the frames make clear and what a camera crew would still need to know before producing the sequence."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain the storyboard as a planning tool that turns a narrative into a sequence of shots. Introduce frame numbering, a simple sketch, action, dialogue or sound notes and a description of what the audience needs to see. Emphasise clear communication rather than artistic drawing skill."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Storyboard the opening of a fictional lost-property story. Model how one action can require more than one frame and how a reaction image helps explain a character’s response. Add notes showing the intended order and discuss whether a viewer could follow the story without a spoken explanation."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students plan a six-frame narrative with an opening situation, a disruption and a plausible resolution. They add concise action and sound notes to each frame. A partner follows the storyboard without hearing the synopsis and identifies a point needing clarification before students revise the plan."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to explain how two adjoining frames connect. Check that the sequence can be followed, the central problem is visible and the ending addresses it. Collect the storyboard and note any missing information for later shot-planning work."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a six-frame template and a simple scenario with the opening already suggested. Encourage stick figures and labelled objects. Use sequencing cards to help students establish the narrative before drawing and annotating their own frames."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students create an alternative opening or ending and explain how it changes audience knowledge or suspense. They justify any deliberate gap between frames instead of leaving an accidental break in the story."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["GCSE Media Studies for AQA (Heinemann): Unit 1 Film: Looking at narratives; Unit 9: Storyboarding","Teacher computer, display screen and speakers where audio is used","Age-appropriate teacher-selected extracts or teacher-prepared fictional media texts","Prepared analysis worksheet, evidence grid or production brief","Paper, pencils, storyboard or design templates, and whiteboard markers","Classroom computers and save folders; camera or approved image bank when required","Lesson success criteria and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-media-studies-t1-w4":{"overview":"Students will develop their understanding of camera framing and composition as choices that direct attention and shape meaning. They will distinguish shot size from camera angle and explain what a particular view reveals or withholds. Students will then produce and annotate a short photographic sequence, using audience feedback to improve its clarity while following classroom equipment and permission routines.","assessment":"Teacher questioning, class discussion, analysis of worked examples and the completed independent activities. Check that students use accurate terminology, select specific evidence and explain how a choice creates meaning or addresses an audience. Review the written, visual or practical work alongside its annotations, and use the final checks in both lessons to identify the next skill requiring practice.","lessons":[{"date":"15 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to identify camera framing and composition choices and explain how they direct audience attention.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Display a wide view and a close-up of the same ordinary object or scene. Ask what each shot allows the viewer to notice and what information becomes unavailable when the frame changes."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Introduce long shot, medium shot, close-up and establishing shot, together with high and low camera angles. Explain framing, subject placement, background and space within the image. Discuss how these choices may suggest a relationship or mood, while avoiding fixed claims that an angle always creates one meaning."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Analyse a teacher-made sequence showing a person noticing a misplaced object. Identify the setting shot, the detail shot and the reaction. Model how changing subject position or the background alters emphasis, and distinguish a camera angle from the distance between camera and subject."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students label a small set of images and explain the purpose of three framing or composition choices. They then sketch two alternative framings of one situation and annotate what the audience would learn from each version. Pairs check that the terms match what is actually visible."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to compare a close-up and a low-angle shot and explain why the terms describe different aspects of an image. Check that their annotations connect a visible choice to audience attention or interpretation."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide labelled shot examples, a distance-and-angle reference and an annotation frame. Let students physically mark the edges of a frame on a printed image before describing the effect. Model one evidence-based explanation."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students compare two compositions that use the same shot size but create different emphasis. They explain how background detail, empty space or subject placement changes the interpretation."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"18 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to create a photographic shot sequence that communicates a simple event through deliberate framing.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Revisit a storyboard from Week 3 and ask which moment needs a wide view and which needs a detail or reaction. Discuss what viewers would misunderstand if every photograph used the same framing."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain how a photographic sequence combines individual shots to communicate an event. Review the approved camera terms and establish a practical routine: plan first, work in the designated area, keep equipment secure and obtain permission before including identifiable people. Use objects or teacher-provided images where permission is unavailable."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Demonstrate a short object-based sequence showing a missing item being found. Capture or arrange an establishing image, a detail and a final revealing image. Check the screen for clarity, distracting backgrounds and continuity before deciding whether another photograph is needed."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Pairs produce a four-to-six-image sequence using varied framing. They arrange the photographs in order and add notes explaining three choices. Another pair describes the event from the images alone; students revise an unclear shot and save the ordered sequence in the class folder."},{"label":"C:","text":"Review whether the sequence communicates the intended event without relying on captions to repair missing images. Ask each student to justify a framing choice and identify one change made after audience feedback."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a simple object-based scenario, a shot checklist and a small set of image placeholders. Assign clear photographer and sequence-planner roles, then exchange roles. Offer a prepared image bank if equipment or permissions prevent original photography."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students create two versions of one shot and compare their effects on audience knowledge. They justify a deliberate use of off-screen space or restricted information while maintaining an understandable sequence."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["GCSE Media Studies for AQA (Heinemann): Unit 1 Film: Audience positioning and camera terms; Unit 9: Making a video","Teacher computer, display screen and speakers where audio is used","Age-appropriate teacher-selected extracts or teacher-prepared fictional media texts","Prepared analysis worksheet, evidence grid or production brief","Paper, pencils, storyboard or design templates, and whiteboard markers","Classroom computers and save folders; camera or approved image bank when required","Lesson success criteria and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-media-studies-t1-w5":{"overview":"Students will investigate how sound and editing work with images to influence meaning and audience response. They will identify dialogue, music, effects, ambient sound and common transitions, connecting these choices to particular moments. Students will develop a written analysis that explains how techniques combine to establish mood or guide attention, using precise evidence and recognising that audience responses may vary.","assessment":"Teacher questioning, class discussion, analysis of worked examples and the completed independent activities. Check that students use accurate terminology, select specific evidence and explain how a choice creates meaning or addresses an audience. Review the written, visual or practical work alongside its annotations, and use the final checks in both lessons to identify the next skill requiring practice.","lessons":[{"date":"22 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to explain how sound and editing choices contribute to meaning and audience response.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Play or describe the same neutral sequence with two different teacher-prepared soundtracks. Ask which details in the images stayed the same and why the suggested mood changed when the sound changed."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Introduce dialogue, music, effects and ambient sound, distinguishing sounds within the story world from music added for the audience. Explain cuts, fades, dissolves and the pace created by shot duration. Discuss sound and editing as choices that guide attention and interpretation, rather than decorative additions."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Use a short age-appropriate sequence to identify a sound cue and a transition. Model a table recording the observable technique, the exact moment and a possible effect. Compare a rapid sequence of cuts with a longer-held shot and explain how the relationship between sound and image matters."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students annotate a prepared sequence using a sound-and-editing log. They identify at least two sound choices and two editing choices, then explain how a combination contributes to mood or suspense. Pairs compare interpretations and improve any statement that only says a technique is “effective”."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to distinguish naming a technique from analysing its effect. Check that their explanations refer to a specific moment and explain how the sound or editing works with the images."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a glossary, a log with one entry completed and a limited set of techniques to locate. Replay short sections separately for sound and image. Use sentence starters linking a technique to a moment and a suggested response."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students explain how an alternative soundtrack or transition could change the same sequence. They evaluate whether a technique reinforces or challenges the expectation established by the images."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"25 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to analyse how sound and editing combine to shape an audience’s experience of a sequence.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Show an annotated analysis that names many techniques but does not explain them. Ask what is missing and how a reader could check whether the claimed audience effect is supported."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain how to build an analysis around a specific moment, a combination of techniques and a reasoned audience response. Distinguish the intended effect from a guaranteed reaction. Encourage students to consider rhythm, the relationship between shots and the information that music or silence supplies."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Model a paragraph about a fictional discovery scene: a detail shot is followed by a reaction while the soundtrack becomes quiet. Explain how the order and sound change focus attention on the discovery. Compare this with an alternative edit that reveals the information earlier."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students independently analyse a second short sequence or a prepared shot-and-sound description. They write two connected paragraphs using precise evidence from both sound and editing. A partner checks the technical terms and highlights any claim requiring a clearer explanation before revision."},{"label":"C:","text":"Invite students to explain how two techniques work together at one moment. Collect their analyses and check for specific evidence, a logical interpretation and qualified audience language rather than a list of disconnected features."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a paragraph structure using technique, evidence, explanation and audience. Offer a timeline of the sequence and prompt students to select one significant moment before attempting a longer response."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students compare two possible edits and judge which better supports a stated purpose. They discuss how an audience with different genre knowledge could interpret the same sound cue differently."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["GCSE Media Studies for AQA (Heinemann): Unit 9: Making a video, post-production, sound and editing","Teacher computer, display screen and speakers where audio is used","Age-appropriate teacher-selected extracts or teacher-prepared fictional media texts","Prepared analysis worksheet, evidence grid or production brief","Paper, pencils, storyboard or design templates, and whiteboard markers","Classroom computers and save folders; camera or approved image bank when required","Lesson success criteria and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-media-studies-t1-w6":{"overview":"Students will develop their understanding of representation as a constructed portrayal based on selection and emphasis. They will identify how appearance, dialogue, action and viewpoint can simplify or complicate a character and examine how stereotyping operates. Students will then support an interpretation with specific evidence, revise unsupported generalisations and consider a plausible alternative reading without endorsing the stereotype being discussed.","assessment":"Teacher questioning, class discussion, analysis of worked examples and the completed independent activities. Check that students use accurate terminology, select specific evidence and explain how a choice creates meaning or addresses an audience. Review the written, visual or practical work alongside its annotations, and use the final checks in both lessons to identify the next skill requiring practice.","lessons":[{"date":"29 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to explain representation and identify how stereotyping can simplify the portrayal of people or groups.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Show two teacher-created descriptions of the same fictional character, each selecting different details. Ask what impression each description creates and which information has been left out."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain representation as the constructed portrayal of people, places or ideas through selected details. Introduce a stereotype as a simplified, repeated set of characteristics associated with a group. Distinguish analysing a portrayal from accepting it as true, and establish respectful language when discussing identity or difference."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Analyse a fictional adult character in a school-based scene through costume, dialogue, action and camera treatment. Identify what the text encourages viewers to notice and what remains unknown. Compare a simplified portrayal with a version that offers the character more agency or complexity."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students annotate two selected fictional character portrayals and identify the choices shaping each impression. They explain whether a portrayal relies on a stereotype and suggest one additional detail that would complicate it. Their response must describe the text without turning a generalisation into a claim about real people."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to explain the difference between a representation and the person or group represented. Check that they refer to selected features and recognise an omission or limitation in the portrayal."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a representation grid covering appearance, dialogue, actions and viewpoint. Use fictional examples with a small number of clear details. Offer sentence stems that describe how the text presents a character without endorsing a stereotype."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students explain how a stereotype could be challenged or used ironically and what evidence would support that reading. They consider whose perspective receives attention and whose is missing."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"2 October 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to support an interpretation of representation with specific evidence and a reasoned explanation.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Present two claims about a fictional character: one supported by a quoted action or visual detail and one based on a personal assumption. Ask students which can be defended from the text and why."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain the relationship between an interpretive claim, precise textual evidence and a reasoned explanation. Discuss alternative readings and the need to distinguish a character’s behaviour from assumptions about a wider group. Encourage students to qualify claims where the extract gives limited information."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Model a paragraph about a character who is first shown in isolation and later helps another person. Explain how framing and action support a changing impression. Compare a persuasive interpretation with one that ignores contradictory evidence and show how the claim can be revised."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students write two evidence-based paragraphs about a teacher-selected portrayal. They use at least one visual detail and one action or line of dialogue. Partners check whether the evidence actually supports the claim, then students revise an overstatement and acknowledge a plausible alternative interpretation."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to identify the strongest evidence in their response and explain its significance. Check that the analysis is about how the portrayal is constructed and that an alternative reading is considered where appropriate."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide an evidence bank and a claim-evidence-explanation paragraph frame. Model how to choose a relevant detail rather than copying every observation. Allow oral discussion before students produce their independent response."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students evaluate two competing interpretations using evidence that supports or weakens each. They explain how the limited viewpoint of an extract affects the confidence of their judgement."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["GCSE Media Studies for AQA (Heinemann): Unit 1 Film and Unit 2 Television: representation and audience positioning","Teacher computer, display screen and speakers where audio is used","Age-appropriate teacher-selected extracts or teacher-prepared fictional media texts","Prepared analysis worksheet, evidence grid or production brief","Paper, pencils, storyboard or design templates, and whiteboard markers","Classroom computers and save folders; camera or approved image bank when required","Lesson success criteria and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-media-studies-t1-w7":{"overview":"Students will consolidate film language, genre, narrative, framing, sound, editing and representation through a mock assessment and a structured review. They will identify the difference between a supported interpretation and a general description, correct their own work and apply the feedback to a new example. The two lessons will prepare students for the internal school assessment by making individual strengths and remaining learning needs clear.","assessment":"Compare each student’s original mock, annotated corrections and new independent response. Check that a correction explains the cause of the problem and that the student can apply the same reasoning to another text or design decision. Record a specific learning need for focused preparation before assessment week.","lessons":[{"date":"6 October 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to complete a mock assessment and identify strengths and gaps in the taught media skills.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Revisit the success criteria for an evidence-based analysis and ask students what a reader needs in addition to the name of a technique. Explain that the mock will identify learning needs before assessment week."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Outline a short mock covering film genre, narrative, camera framing, sound and editing, representation and evidence-based analysis. Explain the difference between independent mock work and the guided review that follows. Students should retain their first responses so that later corrections show how their reasoning has changed."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Read the task requirements together and identify the evidence to submit: a completed analysis, a comparison where required and a short production plan. Check understanding of the instructions without analysing the mock texts or suggesting which interpretation students should choose."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students complete the mock questions and a brief planning task independently. They mark any uncertain response and check that evidence supports each interpretation. Collect their original work, then use the criteria to identify whether the main difficulty concerns terminology, evidence, explanation or meeting the production brief."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to identify one response they can justify and one they need to review. Record individual learning needs from the mock and select anonymous examples for the next lesson, keeping the feedback focused on a specific skill."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide clear task instructions, readable texts and agreed classroom access support. During feedback, use a vocabulary guide or a feature-evidence-meaning table targeted to the misconception. Keep the original independent mock separate from supported corrections."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students identify a plausible alternative interpretation and test it against the available evidence. They explain why an apparently confident response may still be weak if the evidence does not support the claim."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"9 October 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to use feedback to correct a media response and apply the same skill to a different example.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Display an anonymous mock response that describes a feature without explaining it. Ask what is missing and how the writer could connect the observation to meaning, audience or the task brief."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Review the common misconceptions identified in the mock. Distinguish inaccurate terminology, unsupported inference, description without analysis and production choices that do not match the brief. Explain that a useful correction includes the reason for the change and must be tested on another example."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Model a correction using a similar text rather than simply giving the original answer. Add precise evidence, explain its significance and qualify an overstatement. For the production task, show how a proposed change can be checked against the audience or purpose stated in the brief."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students annotate and improve their mock responses, explaining why each significant correction is needed. They then complete a short independent analysis of a new example or a revised design decision. Partners check evidence against the criteria, and students record a specific action for assessment preparation."},{"label":"C:","text":"Compare the original response, the correction and the new independent attempt. Ask students to explain the rule or reasoning they have applied, and record any misconception that still requires a further model before assessment week."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Group students by the learning need shown in their mock. Provide a short worked example, a paragraph frame or an annotation checklist, then remove the scaffold for the new question. Use oral rehearsal to support the written explanation."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students evaluate two possible corrections and justify which responds more directly to the question. They retain a plausible alternative reading where evidence supports it and explain the limitation of their own judgement."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["GCSE Media Studies for AQA (Heinemann): taught Film, Television sections","Teacher computer, display screen and speakers where audio is used","Age-appropriate teacher-selected extracts or teacher-prepared fictional media texts","Prepared mock tasks and feedback criteria","Paper, pencils, storyboard or design templates, and whiteboard markers","Classroom computers and save folders; camera or approved image bank when required","Lesson success criteria and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-media-studies-t1-w8":{"overview":"Students will demonstrate their understanding of film language, genre, narrative, framing, sound, editing and representation through independent internal school analysis and production tasks. They will select relevant evidence, explain the meanings created and justify choices against a stated brief. The assessment will collect individual written responses, a practical production and annotations showing how the taught media skills have been applied.","assessment":"Use the prepared school criteria, independent written responses, practical work and annotations. Assess accurate terminology, relevant evidence, reasoned interpretation and how well the production meets its brief. Record authorised access support and identify the particular analysis or production skills requiring follow-up after assessment.","lessons":[{"date":"13 October 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to demonstrate the taught media-analysis skills through an independent internal school assessment.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Distribute the prepared assessment and check the agreed access arrangements. Ask students to read the instructions and confirm how their individual responses should be recorded."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain the knowledge and analysis requirements and the school criteria. Clarify permitted resources and submission arrangements. Students should select evidence from the supplied texts and explain its significance rather than rely on memorised claims or personal preference."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Check that each student can access the supplied texts. Clarify administrative instructions without interpreting an extract, identifying techniques for students or demonstrating an assessed solution."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students complete the prepared knowledge and analysis questions independently. They identify relevant features, select evidence and explain how choices create meaning or address an audience. Collect individual responses and record authorised support so that the evidence can be assessed fairly against the taught criteria."},{"label":"C:","text":"Collect every response and check for missing pages. Use the school criteria to assess accurate terminology, relevant evidence and explanation. Record specific misconceptions for feedback after assessment."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Apply agreed school access arrangements and clarify administrative instructions only. Record the assistance provided without suggesting an interpretation, supplying a technical term needed for an answer or completing an assessed part of the response."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Within the same task and taught criteria, students may develop a justified alternative reading or a more detailed comparison. Assess the quality of the evidence and reasoning without introducing an untaught theory during assessment."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"16 October 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to demonstrate independent media-production decisions and justify how a design meets a brief.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Check access to the correct production brief and agreed tools. Confirm individual working arrangements, filenames and the evidence required for submission."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain that the practical task requires a short photographic shot sequence or annotated storyboard for a stated purpose. Review the assessment criteria for meeting the brief, deliberate media-language choices, audience or purpose and clear annotations. Students should submit evidence of what they actually produced rather than describe choices that are absent from the work."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Check access to the approved assets, equipment or paper materials. Clarify the evidence and submission requirements without modelling a design or recommending assessed choices."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students independently plan and create the required production using the supplied assets or original work. They annotate their choices and explain how these meet the stated brief. They check the finished product against the requirements, save or submit it and identify a remaining limitation honestly where a feature is incomplete."},{"label":"C:","text":"Collect the production, planning and annotations. Assess purposeful choices, clarity and justification against the brief. Record a specific production or analysis skill requiring further teaching."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Use the agreed access arrangements, accessible tools and administrative help. Provide equivalent access to approved assets or paper materials, while keeping the assessed decisions individual. Record assistance without supplying the design or its justification."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students justify a trade-off between two possible design choices or evaluate an alternative approach within the same brief. Award credit for relevant reasoning and demonstrated quality rather than the amount of extra decoration."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["GCSE Media Studies for AQA (Heinemann): taught Film, Television sections","Teacher computer, display screen and speakers where audio is used","Age-appropriate teacher-selected extracts or teacher-prepared fictional media texts","Prepared internal school assessment papers and individual production briefs","Paper, pencils, storyboard or design templates, and whiteboard markers","Classroom computers and save folders; camera or approved image bank when required","Lesson success criteria and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-media-studies-t1-w10":{"overview":"Students will examine how film posters promote a film by signalling genre, presenting characters and stars and creating audience interest. They will analyse how image, wording and layout combine to address a likely audience. Students will then design a poster for a defined fictional brief, annotate their promotional choices and make a reasoned improvement following peer feedback.","assessment":"Teacher questioning, class discussion, analysis of worked examples and the completed independent activities. Check that students use accurate terminology, select specific evidence and explain how a choice creates meaning or addresses an audience. Review the written, visual or practical work alongside its annotations, and use the final checks in both lessons to identify the next skill requiring practice.","lessons":[{"date":"27 October 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to analyse how film posters promote a film and communicate genre and audience appeal.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Display two film posters with their titles temporarily hidden. Ask what students can infer about genre, characters and likely audience, and which visual or written detail provides the strongest clue."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Introduce the film poster as a promotional text. Explain the roles of the main image, title, tagline, stars, colour, layout and release information. Discuss how these choices work together to create expectations and attract interest, while distinguishing a supported audience inference from a personal preference."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Annotate a teacher-selected age-appropriate poster using the textbook’s film-promotion approach. Identify a prominent feature, explain its suggested meaning and connect it to audience appeal. Compare a second poster to show how a different arrangement or image changes the emphasis."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students analyse two posters in a feature-meaning-audience grid and write a comparative paragraph. They identify at least one shared convention and one significant difference. Each student then proposes a tagline for a fictional film and explains how it supports the intended genre and audience."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to justify an audience claim using two poster features. Check that their response explains promotional choices rather than simply retelling the film’s plot or stating whether they like the design."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a poster-analysis checklist and labelled examples of title, image and tagline. Model one complete entry and encourage students to discuss a possible meaning with a partner before writing independently."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students compare alternative posters for one fictional film or propose two versions for different audiences. They justify how the image, wording or emphasis would need to change."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"30 October 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to design a film poster for a defined audience and justify its promotional choices.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Present a fictional film brief with a stated genre and audience. Ask which features should be most prominent and which choices might confuse the audience about the film being promoted."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain how a design brief guides decisions about image, typography, colour, layout and tagline. Discuss hierarchy and readability so that the title and main message are clear. Review the use of original or teacher-provided assets and explain that the aim is a justified promotional design rather than a finished commercial campaign."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Sketch a poster for a fictional school mystery, identifying the main image, title and supporting information. Explain each decision against the brief and demonstrate how too many competing elements can weaken emphasis. Model an annotation linking a visible design choice to its intended audience effect."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students create a paper or digital poster for the agreed fictional film. They annotate at least four choices and explain how the poster signals genre and addresses the defined audience. A partner reviews the design against the brief; students revise one feature and record the reason for the change."},{"label":"C:","text":"Review the poster and annotations together. Ask students which choice most clearly addresses the audience and what changed after feedback. Check that the design meets the brief and that explanations refer to visible features."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a simple layout template, an approved image bank and a design checklist. Offer a choice of two fictional briefs. Help students establish a clear main image and title before adding supporting information."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students develop an alternative version for a different audience and compare the required changes. They evaluate a trade-off between visual impact, readability and the amount of information included."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["GCSE Media Studies for AQA (Heinemann): Unit 1 Film: How films are promoted, film posters","Teacher computer, display screen and speakers where audio is used","Age-appropriate teacher-selected extracts or teacher-prepared fictional media texts","Prepared analysis worksheet, evidence grid or production brief","Paper, pencils, storyboard or design templates, and whiteboard markers","Classroom computers and save folders; camera or approved image bank when required","Lesson success criteria and teacher observation checklist; check school-event arrangements and reschedule any displaced task"]},"y10-11-media-studies-t1-w11":{"overview":"Students will explore television genres and the ways title sequences introduce a programme’s setting, tone and identity. They will analyse images, sound, editing and graphics to identify the expectations created for an audience. Students will then develop a close analysis of a television opening, explaining how several choices work together and supporting their interpretation with specific moments from the sequence.","assessment":"Teacher questioning, class discussion, analysis of worked examples and the completed independent activities. Check that students use accurate terminology, select specific evidence and explain how a choice creates meaning or addresses an audience. Review the written, visual or practical work alongside its annotations, and use the final checks in both lessons to identify the next skill requiring practice.","lessons":[{"date":"3 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to identify television genre conventions and explain the purposes of a title sequence.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Show two short teacher-selected television openings or prepared still sequences. Ask what viewers can infer before the main programme begins and which clues suggest the type of programme."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Introduce television genres through recurring settings, characters, formats and audience expectations. Explain the title sequence as a combination of images, music, graphics and names that introduces a programme and establishes its identity. Distinguish the opening’s promotional role from a summary of everything that happens in an episode."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Analyse a fictional school-drama opening using a feature-and-inference table. Identify location images, the title style and the music, then explain how they suggest setting, tone and likely narrative concerns. Compare a contrasting opening to show how genre expectations shape the choices."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students classify two openings and support each genre judgement with three features. They identify what each title sequence reveals and what it withholds. Each student writes a short explanation of how one combination of image, sound and typography establishes an expectation for the audience."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to distinguish a genre convention from a feature used by only one programme. Check that their explanation links a title-sequence detail to an inference about tone, setting or audience."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a genre word bank and an opening-analysis table divided into image, sound, graphics and inference. Replay short sections and model one connection before asking students to identify a different feature."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students explain how a title sequence could blend conventions from two genres. They discuss how familiarity with a recurring opening may help build programme recognition."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"6 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to analyse how a television opening combines technical choices to introduce a programme to an audience.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Return to an opening with its sound removed. Ask what can still be inferred and which expectations become less certain when the soundtrack is unavailable."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain how an analysis should connect camera choices, editing, sound and graphics rather than treating them as unrelated features. Discuss the order of information and how an opening may position viewers toward a place or character. Remind students that audience effects need evidence and may vary between viewers."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Model a close analysis of the first three moments of a prepared opening. Record the observable details, explain an inference and show how the next moment confirms or complicates it. Demonstrate a paragraph that refers to both image and sound and supports a judgement about the programme’s tone."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students complete a timeline of a selected television opening and write two analytical paragraphs. They refer to at least three technical choices and explain how these establish genre or audience expectations. Partners identify an unsupported claim, and students improve it using more precise evidence."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to explain which moment gives the clearest clue about the programme and why. Check that the completed analysis uses accurate terms and links a combination of choices to meaning."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a partly completed timeline, still images from the opening and sentence starters. Encourage students to focus on a few significant moments rather than attempting to record every cut or sound."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students propose a change to the opening and explain how it would alter expectations. They compare their reading with a plausible alternative and assess which details support each."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["GCSE Media Studies for AQA (Heinemann): Unit 2 Television: Title sequences and television genres","Teacher computer, display screen and speakers where audio is used","Age-appropriate teacher-selected extracts or teacher-prepared fictional media texts","Prepared analysis worksheet, evidence grid or production brief","Paper, pencils, storyboard or design templates, and whiteboard markers","Classroom computers and save folders; camera or approved image bank when required","Lesson success criteria and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-media-studies-t1-w12":{"overview":"Students will examine how news language and news values influence which stories are selected and how they are presented. They will distinguish the shared facts of an event from the angle suggested by a headline or lead. Students will compare two teacher-prepared newspaper accounts of one fictional event, using precise evidence to explain differences in emphasis without making unsupported claims about intention.","assessment":"Teacher questioning, class discussion, analysis of worked examples and the completed independent activities. Check that students use accurate terminology, select specific evidence and explain how a choice creates meaning or addresses an audience. Review the written, visual or practical work alongside its annotations, and use the final checks in both lessons to identify the next skill requiring practice.","lessons":[{"date":"10 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to explain how news language and news values influence the selection and presentation of a story.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Offer a set of fictional school-news summaries and ask students which should lead a school newspaper. Compare their choices and ask what makes a story seem important to that audience."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Introduce news values as considerations that influence selection, such as relevance, scale, recency, conflict and human interest. Explain the functions of headlines, leads, quotation and attribution. Distinguish a reported fact from an opinion or unverified claim and avoid assuming that news selection is a complete record of events."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Use a fictional report about a new school library to identify a headline, a lead and an attributed comment. Compare a neutral headline with a more evaluative version. Explain how word choice and emphasis can suggest an angle while the underlying event remains the same."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students rank the prepared story summaries for a defined audience and justify their first two choices using news values. They then rewrite one headline and lead in two styles, identifying which words change the emphasis and ensuring that the supplied facts are not altered."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to distinguish the event from the angle taken on it. Check that their news-value explanation considers the intended audience and that quotations or claims are attributed rather than presented as unsupported facts."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a news-value glossary, a labelled article and a fact-and-opinion sorting task. Model how to justify one story choice before students rank the remaining examples and explain their own decisions."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students compare the priorities of two different audiences and explain how the leading story might change. They identify information missing from the supplied report that a journalist would need to verify."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"13 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to compare how two newspaper reports present the same event through language and selection.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Display two teacher-prepared headlines about the same fictional school event. Ask what each makes the reader notice first and whether either headline tells the whole story."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain how to compare headline wording, image selection, lead information, quotation and prominence across two reports. Distinguish an observable difference from an assumption about a writer’s intention. Discuss omission and emphasis while recognising that two short reports may differ for reasons that the available evidence cannot establish."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Model a comparison of two fictional reports about a school garden opening. Identify a shared fact, a different emphasis and an attributed viewpoint. Write a paragraph that refers to both reports and explains how a specific wording choice may shape the reader’s impression."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students annotate the paired reports and complete a comparison grid. They write two paragraphs addressing the same feature in both texts, using precise language evidence. A partner checks the accuracy of the shared facts and highlights a claim that needs qualification or stronger support."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to explain one difference without claiming more than the texts support. Check that the analysis compares the same event, identifies evidence in both reports and distinguishes language choices from factual differences."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide short paired reports, highlighted factual details and a comparison frame. Model a single headline comparison and allow students to rehearse an explanation orally before writing the independent response."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students explain how an omitted viewpoint might affect the account and propose a question for further reporting. They evaluate which report gives a more complete account using the supplied evidence rather than personal agreement."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["GCSE Media Studies for AQA (Heinemann): Unit 3 Newspapers: Newspaper language, news values and audiences","Teacher computer, display screen and speakers where audio is used","Age-appropriate teacher-selected extracts or teacher-prepared fictional media texts","Prepared analysis worksheet, evidence grid or production brief","Paper, pencils, storyboard or design templates, and whiteboard markers","Classroom computers and save folders; camera or approved image bank when required","Lesson success criteria and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-media-studies-t1-w13":{"overview":"Students will analyse magazine-cover conventions and the relationship between design, content promises and audience interests. They will examine how the masthead, main image and cover lines establish hierarchy and magazine identity. Students will then create and annotate an original cover for a stated audience, justify their choices and revise a feature using feedback against the brief.","assessment":"Teacher questioning, class discussion, analysis of worked examples and the completed independent activities. Check that students use accurate terminology, select specific evidence and explain how a choice creates meaning or addresses an audience. Review the written, visual or practical work alongside its annotations, and use the final checks in both lessons to identify the next skill requiring practice.","lessons":[{"date":"17 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to analyse magazine-cover conventions and explain how they address a defined audience.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Display two contrasting teacher-selected magazine covers. Ask what interests each cover promises to satisfy and which design or language features suggest its intended readership."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Introduce the masthead, main image, cover lines, colour palette, typography, price and issue information. Explain how layout creates hierarchy and how a cover communicates a magazine’s identity. Discuss audience interests and lifestyle as evidence-based inferences, avoiding unsupported stereotypes about who must read a particular magazine."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Annotate a fictional student-technology magazine cover. Identify the most prominent elements and explain how the main image and cover lines work together to promise useful content. Compare a contrasting cover and distinguish features common to the form from choices specific to its audience."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students annotate two covers and produce an audience profile supported by at least three visible features. They write one comparative paragraph explaining how language and design address different interests. Pairs check that every audience claim is linked to evidence rather than an assumption about the reader."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to distinguish the magazine’s title from a cover line and explain the purpose of each. Check that the analysis considers hierarchy and audience appeal as well as identifying conventions."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a labelled cover, a conventions checklist and an audience-evidence grid. Model one connection between a cover line and a reader interest. Encourage students to focus on the main image and headline before analysing smaller details."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students identify a possible secondary audience or a tension between two cover elements. They explain how changing one convention could alter the magazine’s identity or perceived readership."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"20 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to create and annotate a magazine cover that uses conventions to address a stated audience.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Present a brief for a fictional magazine aimed at students with a shared interest. Ask what content promise should be most visible and how a reader could recognise the magazine on a crowded shelf or screen."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain how a cover design translates an audience brief into a main image, masthead, cover lines and a clear visual hierarchy. Discuss readable typography, limited competing elements and consistency of tone. Review original or teacher-provided assets and remind students that annotations must justify actual choices in their design."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Sketch two possible layouts for a fictional school-creativity magazine. Compare their hierarchy and choose one against the brief. Model a specific annotation explaining how a cover line promises relevant content and how its position helps the intended reader notice it."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students create a paper or digital cover with a masthead, main image and at least three purposeful cover lines. They annotate four choices using the audience brief, then exchange designs for a conventions-and-audience check. Students revise one feature and explain why the change improves the cover."},{"label":"C:","text":"Collect the design, annotations and brief. Ask students to justify the most important cover choice and identify evidence that it addresses the intended audience. Check that the annotations explain the visible design rather than describing an unrealised intention."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a cover template, an approved image bank and a short audience brief. Use an annotation frame linking choice, purpose and audience. Help students establish one clear focal point before adding secondary cover lines."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students create a second layout or adapt the cover for a different readership. They explain the trade-off between preserving magazine identity and changing the language or design to address a new audience."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["GCSE Media Studies for AQA (Heinemann): Unit 4 Magazines: Analysing magazine covers and describing audiences","Teacher computer, display screen and speakers where audio is used","Age-appropriate teacher-selected extracts or teacher-prepared fictional media texts","Prepared analysis worksheet, evidence grid or production brief","Paper, pencils, storyboard or design templates, and whiteboard markers","Classroom computers and save folders; camera or approved image bank when required","Lesson success criteria and teacher observation checklist"]},"y10-11-media-studies-t1-w14":{"overview":"Students will consolidate film language, genre, narrative, framing, sound, editing and representation, with the taught poster, television, news and magazine work through a mock assessment and a structured review. They will identify the difference between a supported interpretation and a general description, correct their own work and apply the feedback to a new example. The two lessons will prepare students for the internal school assessment by making individual strengths and remaining learning needs clear.","assessment":"Compare each student’s original mock, annotated corrections and new independent response. Check that a correction explains the cause of the problem and that the student can apply the same reasoning to another text or design decision. Record a specific learning need for focused preparation before assessment week.","lessons":[{"date":"24 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to complete a mock assessment and identify strengths and gaps in the taught media skills.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Revisit the success criteria for an evidence-based analysis and ask students what a reader needs in addition to the name of a technique. Explain that the mock will identify learning needs before assessment week."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Outline a short mock covering film genre, narrative, camera framing, sound and editing, representation and evidence-based analysis, film promotion, television openings, news and magazine audiences. Explain the difference between independent mock work and the guided review that follows. Students should retain their first responses so that later corrections show how their reasoning has changed."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Read the task requirements together and identify the evidence to submit: a completed analysis, a comparison where required and a short production plan. Check understanding of the instructions without analysing the mock texts or suggesting which interpretation students should choose."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students complete the mock questions and a brief planning task independently. They mark any uncertain response and check that evidence supports each interpretation. Collect their original work, then use the criteria to identify whether the main difficulty concerns terminology, evidence, explanation or meeting the production brief."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to identify one response they can justify and one they need to review. Record individual learning needs from the mock and select anonymous examples for the next lesson, keeping the feedback focused on a specific skill."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide clear task instructions, readable texts and agreed classroom access support. During feedback, use a vocabulary guide or a feature-evidence-meaning table targeted to the misconception. Keep the original independent mock separate from supported corrections."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students identify a plausible alternative interpretation and test it against the available evidence. They explain why an apparently confident response may still be weak if the evidence does not support the claim."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"27 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to use feedback to correct a media response and apply the same skill to a different example.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Display an anonymous mock response that describes a feature without explaining it. Ask what is missing and how the writer could connect the observation to meaning, audience or the task brief."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Review the common misconceptions identified in the mock. Distinguish inaccurate terminology, unsupported inference, description without analysis and production choices that do not match the brief. Explain that a useful correction includes the reason for the change and must be tested on another example."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Model a correction using a similar text rather than simply giving the original answer. Add precise evidence, explain its significance and qualify an overstatement. For the production task, show how a proposed change can be checked against the audience or purpose stated in the brief."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students annotate and improve their mock responses, explaining why each significant correction is needed. They then complete a short independent analysis of a new example or a revised design decision. Partners check evidence against the criteria, and students record a specific action for assessment preparation."},{"label":"C:","text":"Compare the original response, the correction and the new independent attempt. Ask students to explain the rule or reasoning they have applied, and record any misconception that still requires a further model before assessment week."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Group students by the learning need shown in their mock. Provide a short worked example, a paragraph frame or an annotation checklist, then remove the scaffold for the new question. Use oral rehearsal to support the written explanation."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students evaluate two possible corrections and justify which responds more directly to the question. They retain a plausible alternative reading where evidence supports it and explain the limitation of their own judgement."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["GCSE Media Studies for AQA (Heinemann): taught Film, Television, Newspapers and Magazines sections","Teacher computer, display screen and speakers where audio is used","Age-appropriate teacher-selected extracts or teacher-prepared fictional media texts","Prepared mock tasks and feedback criteria","Paper, pencils, storyboard or design templates, and whiteboard markers","Classroom computers and save folders; camera or approved image bank when required","Lesson success criteria and teacher observation checklist; check school-event arrangements and reschedule any displaced task"]},"y10-11-media-studies-t1-w15":{"overview":"Students will demonstrate their understanding of film language, genre, narrative, framing, sound, editing and representation, with the taught poster, television, news and magazine work through independent internal school analysis and production tasks. They will select relevant evidence, explain the meanings created and justify choices against a stated brief. The assessment will collect individual written responses, a practical production and annotations showing how the taught media skills have been applied.","assessment":"Use the prepared school criteria, independent written responses, practical work and annotations. Assess accurate terminology, relevant evidence, reasoned interpretation and how well the production meets its brief. Record authorised access support and identify the particular analysis or production skills requiring follow-up after assessment.","lessons":[{"date":"1 December 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to demonstrate the taught media-analysis skills through an independent internal school assessment.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Distribute the prepared assessment and check the agreed access arrangements. Ask students to read the instructions and confirm how their individual responses should be recorded."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain the knowledge and analysis requirements and the school criteria. Clarify permitted resources and submission arrangements. Students should select evidence from the supplied texts and explain its significance rather than rely on memorised claims or personal preference."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Check that each student can access the supplied texts. Clarify administrative instructions without interpreting an extract, identifying techniques for students or demonstrating an assessed solution."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students complete the prepared knowledge and analysis questions independently. They identify relevant features, select evidence and explain how choices create meaning or address an audience. Collect individual responses and record authorised support so that the evidence can be assessed fairly against the taught criteria."},{"label":"C:","text":"Collect every response and check for missing pages. Use the school criteria to assess accurate terminology, relevant evidence and explanation. Record specific misconceptions for feedback after assessment."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Apply agreed school access arrangements and clarify administrative instructions only. Record the assistance provided without suggesting an interpretation, supplying a technical term needed for an answer or completing an assessed part of the response."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Within the same task and taught criteria, students may develop a justified alternative reading or a more detailed comparison. Assess the quality of the evidence and reasoning without introducing an untaught theory during assessment."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"4 December 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to demonstrate independent media-production decisions and justify how a design meets a brief.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Check access to the correct production brief and agreed tools. Confirm individual working arrangements, filenames and the evidence required for submission."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain that the practical task requires a magazine cover or film poster for a defined audience. Review the assessment criteria for meeting the brief, deliberate media-language choices, audience or purpose and clear annotations. Students should submit evidence of what they actually produced rather than describe choices that are absent from the work."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Check access to the approved assets, equipment or paper materials. Clarify the evidence and submission requirements without modelling a design or recommending assessed choices."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students independently plan and create the required production using the supplied assets or original work. They annotate their choices and explain how these meet the stated brief. They check the finished product against the requirements, save or submit it and identify a remaining limitation honestly where a feature is incomplete."},{"label":"C:","text":"Collect the production, planning and annotations. Assess purposeful choices, clarity and justification against the brief. Record a specific production or analysis skill requiring further teaching."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Use the agreed access arrangements, accessible tools and administrative help. Provide equivalent access to approved assets or paper materials, while keeping the assessed decisions individual. Record assistance without supplying the design or its justification."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students justify a trade-off between two possible design choices or evaluate an alternative approach within the same brief. Award credit for relevant reasoning and demonstrated quality rather than the amount of extra decoration."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["GCSE Media Studies for AQA (Heinemann): taught Film, Television, Newspapers and Magazines sections","Teacher computer, display screen and speakers where audio is used","Age-appropriate teacher-selected extracts or teacher-prepared fictional media texts","Prepared internal school assessment papers and individual production briefs","Paper, pencils, storyboard or design templates, and whiteboard markers","Classroom computers and save folders; camera or approved image bank when required","Lesson success criteria and teacher observation checklist; check school-event arrangements and reschedule any displaced task"]}});
